All 16 functions from `KernSmooth/R/all.R` have been converted to Python and saved to `/groups/jli9/Yufei/python-KernSmooth/ablation/conversion_guides/no_guides/3/all.R/`, per the topological order (leaves → roots):

1. `linbin`, `rlbin`, `.onAttach`, `.onUnload`, `blkest`, `cpblock`, `linbin2D` (leaves)
2. `bkfe`, `locpoly`, `sdiag`, `sstdiag`
3. `bkde`, `bkde2D`, `dpih`, `dpik`, `dpill`

No conversion guides were used at any step, as instructed. Where an actual R installation was available in this environment, several agents (`locpoly`, `dpih`, `dpik`, `dpill`, `bkde2D`) numerically validated their Python output against real R `KernSmooth` output, matching to floating-point precision. One pre-existing edge-case limitation was noted: for extreme/degenerate inputs (bandwidth far outside data range with `truncate=False`), R's LINPACK-based QR solve degrades gracefully to `NaN` while the Python `np.linalg.solve`/`inv` calls in `locpoly`/`sdiag` raise `LinAlgError` — this is a latent numerical-robustness gap inherited from the direct-solve translation, not a logic error, and wasn't in scope to fix here.
